libdtfl NEWS -- user-visible changes, newest first.

Version 0.1.2 (unreleased)

	Fix: an arrangement brought into a nested scope with
	dtfl_arranged_enter returned wrong times when read through
	dtfl_arranged_join_core, dtfl_arranged_reduce_core or
	dtfl_arranged_reduce_abelian.  Its trace handle shared the outer
	trace's batches without lifting their times into the inner scope,
	so the operator read each update's inner coordinate from the next
	update's time, and the last one from uninitialised memory.  A join
	inside an iterate over an entered arrangement, as in the
	reachability server plugin, emitted updates at wrong iterations.
	Every release up to 0.1.1 has the bug.  The entered handle now reads
	the shared trace through a port of upstream's TraceEnter.

	Platforms.  On Alpine 3.24 (musl 1.2.6) with gcc 15.2 and clang
	22.1: make check with hegel, under ASan and UBSan, and under UBSan in
	trap mode, all 129 tests passing and 2 skipped (USDT, libxtc) in each
	of the six builds; valgrind memcheck over the test programs, with no
	error and no definite leak in the 81 that finish under it (the 24
	property tests crash inside libhegel under valgrind, and 5 slow
	tests hit their time limits).  ThreadSanitizer on musl finds
	no race but fails 17 tests under clang and 30 under gcc, because its
	musl runtime cannot longjmp after a thread starts and gcc's also
	aborts on large TLS blocks; the manual's platform notes say more.
	On FreeBSD 14.5 with hegel 0.36.5 built from source: make check with
	the property tests under clang 21 and gcc 14, ASan and UBSan under
	both, and TSan under clang, 129 passing and 2 skipped each; the
	manual gives the build recipe.  macOS was not run for this release:
	no macOS host was available.

	Linux riscv64.  On Bianbu 4.0.7 (Ubuntu 26.04 base, Linux 6.18,
	a SpacemiT X100 with 8 cores), with gcc 15.2 and clang 21.1: make
	check with hegel, under ASan and UBSan, and under TSan, all 130
	tests passing under each compiler; make check without hegel and
	make distcheck under both; under gcc, the heavy explain sweep, the
	longrun test and make check-sim with 100,000 seeds for each of its
	six properties; and the interop tier, with dev/gen-fixtures --check
	regenerating all five fixtures from the pinned Rust crates byte for
	byte.  hegel publishes no riscv64 libhegel, so these runs used
	hegel 0.36.5 built from source with the distribution's Rust 1.93;
	the manual's platform notes say how.  The TSan runtime on
	riscv64 aborts under setarch -R, which configure used to apply to
	every Linux TSan build, so configure now tries a sanitizer program
	under its test wrapper and drops the wrapper when it fails there
	and runs without it.  No library code changed.  Not run: valgrind,
	which cannot start any program on this system (valgrind 3.26 does
	not decode the Zba instructions in the system's dynamic loader),
	and the benchmarks.

	make check's summary counted 11 test scripts twice, so TOTAL said
	141 for 130 tests.  It now counts each test once, and make check
	fails if a test is listed twice.

	The libxtc example in dtfl_set_sync_hooks(3) now runs, as
	examples/embed/xtc_runtime (configure --with-libxtc), with 4
	worker fibers under dtfl_execute and 4 under dtfl_execute_worker on
	1, 2 and 4 carriers.  The page is corrected for libxtc 2.0: its
	xtc_pid_t is 16 bytes, so the self hook packs the loop and slot;
	yield and sleep fall back when called off a fiber; and it now says
	what the hooks do not reach: a worker that waits by looping on
	dtfl_worker_step holds its carrier, and dtfl_transport_socket's
	poll(2) blocks it unless the host moves the wait off the carrier.

	Benchmarks rerun at 66154af, five alternated reps per point; the
	README, the manual's Threads chapter and
	dev/notes/pg-incremental-views.md now give these figures.  On the
	two-socket c6i.metal (dev/bench-ec2, full matrix), C's 16-worker
	arrange throughput rose from 69.4M to 83.8M records/s packed and
	from 56.3M to 72.7M spread, while Rust's stayed within noise, so C
	is 1.11x and 1.05x slower where it was 1.34x and 1.39x (and 1.24x
	where it was 1.39x at 4 workers packed).  The count arm gained 2%
	to 7% at 4 and 16 workers (1.39x to 1.33x at 16 packed), and one
	worker is unchanged.  On a c7i.metal-24xl, rerunning
	the build each old figure came from beside 66154af:
	bench/ivm_latency in the difference form is about 5% faster, 104 /
	130 us (p50 / p99) at 1,000 commits/s with 16 groups where it was
	111 / 138, and p99 184 us where it was 192 with 1,000 groups; with
	reduce at 1,000 groups, p99 rose from 1.22-1.25 ms to 1.35-1.45
	ms.  bench/idle_merge's longest idle step on a 10M-key trace is 16
	to 23 ms over effort 1e3 to 1e5, where it was 13 to 20 ms; at
	effort 1e4 it went from 14.4-15.0 ms at a8e7815 to 19.5-19.7 ms.
	The event_driven example is unchanged at 1.24x Rust's time (100
	dataflows of 100 maps) and 1.22x (10 of 1000).

	A libFuzzer tier.  ./configure CC=clang --enable-fuzz, then make
	fuzz, fuzzes eleven targets covering every decoder of untrusted
	bytes: TCP message framing, data and progress messages, capture
	events, times, log records, recorded log streams and dtfl-diag,
	the WebSocket handshake, DDIR programs and values, and record
	descriptors.  Each target replays its committed corpus in
	tests/corpus/fuzz/ first.  Fixes from its first campaign: the
	capture EventReader no longer reads past its buffer on a frame
	length near 2^64; dtfl_log_operates_addr and
	dtfl_log_channels_scope return a valid pointer, not NULL, for an
	empty address; a DDIR integer literal past i64 is a parse error
	instead of signed overflow.  Still open: a DDIR program or value
	with an unresolved name, an unknown scope or an out-of-range
	variable makes dtfl_ddir_parse and dtfl_ddir_value_parse panic
	(abort by default) instead of returning an error, so dtfl-ddir
	server cannot yet keep running on such input.

Version 0.1.1 (2026-10-24)

	Packaging fix for 0.1.0; the library is unchanged.

	The 0.1.0 tag was never published: the release workflow's test of
	the installed tarball asked pkg-config for the configured prefix
	instead of the staging directory, so its consumer program could not
	find the headers.  The test now points pkg-config at the staging
	directory.  The source is the 0.1.0 source with the version number
	raised, so 0.1.0's qualification below covers it.

Version 0.1.0 (2026-10-24)

	First development release: timely 0.31.0 and differential 0.25.1 in C11.

	Qualification.  At the release commit, on Debian 13 amd64: make
	check with hegel, under ASan and UBSan, under TSan, with clang 19,
	under clang UBSan, and the heavy explain sweep; make distcheck; make
	check-sim with 100,000 seeds for each of its six properties; the
	interop tier, with dev/gen-fixtures --check regenerating the
	fixtures from the pinned Rust crates byte for byte; and the docs
	gate, with all 79 man pages clean under mandoc -Tlint.  On Debian 13
	arm64: make check with hegel under gcc 14 and clang 19, ASan and
	UBSan and TSan under both, and make distcheck under both.  On FreeBSD
	14.5 amd64: make check and make distcheck under clang 21 and gcc 14,
	ASan and UBSan under both, and TSan under clang.  With musl-gcc on
	Debian 13: make check and make distcheck.  On GitHub CI: Ubuntu
	24.04 with gcc and clang, each plain, with ASan and UBSan and with
	TSan, plus valgrind on a subset and make distcheck; musl; macOS 26
	arm64; and FreeBSD 15.1 in a VM.  The full metal benchmark ran on a
	c7i.metal-24xl at 944d6d3, which differs from the release only in
	the portability fixes listed below, none of them on a path the
	benchmark runs.  The README's tables come from that run, and every
	figure is within run-to-run noise of the previous run.  Not run:
	the libFuzzer tier (make fuzz has no target yet); the property
	tests on FreeBSD and macOS (hegel publishes no libhegel there); the
	sanitizers on macOS and musl; Linux riscv64 and the two-socket
	benchmark, which last ran on earlier builds; and the event_driven,
	idle-merge and IVM latency figures, which the benchmark matrix does
	not include.

	Workers in processes, and a host runtime.  dtfl_execute_worker
	joins the calling thread to an execution as worker i of n over
	a dtfl_transport, the byte stream the cluster's network layer
	is built on (upstream's Stream trait), and starts no thread:
	the worker's own steps move its bytes and it parks in the
	transport.  This lets PostgreSQL background workers share one
	computation over shm_mq, and lets any host connect processes it
	created.  dtfl_transport_socket is a ready-made transport over
	connected sockets, and dtfl_config_set_workers with
	DTFL_WORKERS_PROCESSES makes dtfl_execute fork its workers over
	a socketpair mesh.  dtfl_set_sync_hooks, a fifth host hook,
	routes every lock and wait libdtfl makes (mutexes, condition
	variables, the word wait the parker sleeps on, yield, sleep, the
	clock and the thread identity) through the host, so a fiber
	runtime can keep its carriers out of the kernel.  The defaults
	are unchanged.  dtfl_set_error_detail lets a host callback,
	such as a transport returning DTFL_EIO, leave the reason that
	dtfl_last_error_detail and the panic message report.
	examples/embed/forked_workers shows the
	process pattern; dtfl_embedding(7) and the manual's Embedding
	chapter cover each PostgreSQL concurrency model and fiber hosts.

	A C11 port of timely dataflow (pinned at timely-v0.31.0),
	differential dataflow (differential-dataflow-v0.25.1), and the
	timely diagnostics tools.  Functionally complete: the timely
	runtime and all operators across five execution modes (single
	thread, threads, processes, simulated, and a TCP cluster);
	differential collections, traces, and shared arrangements; join,
	reduce, iterate, count, threshold, and distinct; the graph
	algorithms and the dogsdogsdogs worst-case-optimal joins; the
	diagnostics WebSocket server with the dtfl-diag program; and the
	DDIR dataflow compiler with the dtfl-ddir and dtfl-server tools.

	Thread-safe, with allocation, panic, logging, thread and sync
	hooks for embedding (for example in PostgreSQL).  A Texinfo
	manual and 79 man pages, both gate-checked.

	Portability.  The full suite, with ASan, UBSan and TSan, passes on
	FreeBSD 14 amd64 (clang 21 and gcc 14) and on Linux arm64 (gcc 14
	and clang 19), as well as Linux x86_64.  FreeBSD fixes: the build
	with gcc and GNU ld no longer falls back to the Apple
	exported-symbols list (configure's version-script probe hid
	environ, which crt1.o exports); two sources that used usleep and
	INADDR_LOOPBACK, which POSIX.1-2008 lacks, now build.  On FreeBSD a
	sanitizer build's tests run under proccontrol -m aslr -s disable
	(gcc's libasan will not start with ASLR on), tests/tsan.supp
	silences a libthr-internal TSan report, and configure stops on
	--enable-tsan with gcc, which has no TSan runtime there.  The USDT
	probes are off on FreeBSD, whose sys/sdt.h is the kernel's.  On
	macOS, which has no pthread_condattr_setclock, a timed condition
	wait is relative (pthread_cond_timedwait_relative_np), and the
	test scripts run without timeout(1) when it is missing.  With
	musl-gcc, which has no kernel headers, a parked worker sleeps on a
	condition variable instead of a futex.  On macOS and the BSDs the
	diagnostics server no longer drops a client whose WebSocket
	request arrives after the server accepted the connection: the
	accepted socket inherited the listener's O_NONBLOCK there, so the
	handshake read failed with EAGAIN.  See "Platform notes" in the
	manual.

	Embedding fixes.  The allocation hooks never receive a NULL
	pointer in realloc_fn or free_fn: an empty buffer grows through
	malloc_fn.  The PostgreSQL hooks in dtfl_embedding(7) used
	repalloc, which crashes on NULL, and palloc, which refuses more
	than 1 GB; they now use MemoryContextAllocHuge and repalloc_huge
	in a dedicated memory context, and examples/embed enforces the
	server's contract so a broken hook fails make check.  The manual
	and dtfl_set_panic_hook(3) now say what a longjmping panic hook
	leaves behind: the worker is unusable and must be discarded, for
	example by resetting its memory context.  Thread hooks must start
	threads in the same process, so a PostgreSQL background worker
	cannot back them; the earlier documentation said it could.
	man dtfl_set_panic_hook now finds its page.

	Every operator that upstream builds on Operator::unary or ::binary
	no longer runs on every frontier change, only when data arrives, as
	upstream's FrontierInterest::Never makes it.  Among them: timely's
	flat_map, filter and exchange; differential's map, flat_map,
	explode, negate, enter, enter_at, leave, delay, results_in,
	as_collection and the arranged enter and leave; dogsdogsdogs'
	differentiate and integrate; and DDIR's linear operator.
	Advancing an input past a chain of such operators no longer costs
	time in the length of the chain: examples/timely/event_driven at
	10 dataflows of 1000 maps runs 1000 rounds in about 0.1 s where it
	took 1.4 s.

	dtfl_config_set_idle_merge_effort (differential's
	Config::idle_merge_effort) lets arrangement, reduce and upsert
	operators keep merging their traces while their input is idle,
	until each trace is a single batch.  Off by default.

	dtfl_trace_stats_get reports a trace's layers, batches, pending
	batches, merges in progress, updates and bytes, for monitoring.

	make check-sim runs the multi-worker property tests under the
	simulator with drawn seeds, worker stalls and delivery delays that
	keep each channel's FIFO order; DTFL_SIM_SEEDS and DTFL_SIM_JOBS
	size the run (see the manual's Installation chapter).

	Fixed a hang inherited from timely 0.31.0 (timely-dataflow issue
	#824): if an input that feeds an exchange was advanced, sent to,
	flushed, its worker stepped, and the input then closed with
	nothing sent since, the records still buffered in the exchange
	were never sent and the dataflow never completed.  Advancing
	instead of closing delayed them until the next send.  The pushing
	Counter now forwards a flush whenever data went through it since
	the last flush, where timely forwards one only while the
	operator's produced counts are undrained.  This is a deliberate
	divergence from the pin; see dev/upstream-notes.md.

	The input operator no longer asks to be scheduled when it is built,
	as upstream's does not.  It used to, which made the hang above
	reachable without an advance.

	USDT static probes under the provider dtfl, for DTrace, SystemTap,
	bpftrace and perf: operator_start and operator_end, park,
	park_end and unpark, batch_seal, and merge_begin and merge_end.
	configure builds them when sys/sdt.h is present (--enable-usdt,
	--disable-usdt); otherwise they compile to nothing.  The manual's
	Diagnostics chapter lists their arguments.

	The library keeps frame pointers by default, release builds
	included, so profilers can walk its stack without DWARF unwinding.
	--disable-frame-pointers turns this off.

	Throughput within 1.5 times of the Rust originals on the pinned
	arrange benchmark at every worker count measured, 1 to 48, on a
	dedicated bare-metal host with the clock pinned: 0.91 to 1.16 times
	on the arrange arm and 1.00 to 1.30 times on count, matching Rust at
	48 workers.  A reserved batch is one allocation; messages,
	capabilities, sessions and received buffers come from worker-owned
	free lists, so allocation calls per record are 1.10 (arrange) and
	1.35 (count) times Rust's, and a buffer freed by the worker that
	received it no longer makes glibc trim the sender's arena.

	bench/idle_merge times the longest idle step with idle merge effort
	(13 to 20 ms on a 10M-key trace, mostly freeing merged inputs), and
	bench/ivm_latency the commit-to-delta latency of a maintained view.
	dev/bench-ec2 takes --placement packed|spread for two-socket hosts.

	bench/experiments/arrange is a faithful port of arrange.rs: it
	takes upstream's arguments (keys recs rate work comp, then timely
	options and open-loop), and prints the same throughput and latency
	lines in the closed-loop and the open-loop mode.
	dtfl_collection_inner returns the update stream behind a
	collection (Collection::inner).
